<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"> <head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Multi-Level Decks &amp; Pergolas in Central MA | MCB</title><meta name="description" content="Multi-level decks, open-top pergolas, built-in benches and planters in Central Massachusetts: sloped-lot design, code points and typical installed costs."><link rel="canonical" href="https://mcitybuilders.com/deck-building/multi-level-decks-and-pergolas"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Multi-Level Decks &amp; Pergolas in Central MA | MCB"><meta property="og:description" content="Multi-level decks, open-top pergolas, built-in benches and planters in Central Massachusetts: sloped-lot design, code points and typical installed costs."><meta property="og:url" content="https://mcitybuilders.com/deck-building/multi-level-decks-and-pergolas"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Maverick City Builders"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Manrope:wght@200;300;400;500;600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"GeneralContractor","@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business","name":"Maverick City Builders","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com","telephone":"(508) 656-7436","email":"douglas@mcitybuilders.com","address":{"@type":"PostalAddress","streetAddress":"1171 Main St","addressLocality":"Lancaster","addressRegion":"MA","postalCode":"01523","addressCountry":"US"},"geo":{"@type":"GeoCoordinates","latitude":42.4556,"longitude":-71.6731},"sameAs":["https://www.facebook.com/mcitybuilders","https://www.instagram.com/mcitybuilders"]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Decks","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/deck-building"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Multi-Level Decks and Pergolas","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/deck-building/multi-level-decks-and-pergolas"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","serviceType":"Multi-Level Decks and Pergolas","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com/deck-building/multi-level-decks-and-pergolas","provider":{"@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"AdministrativeArea","name":"Central Massachusetts"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Why build two levels instead of one big deck?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a sloped lot, a single deck at door height ends up high off the ground at the far end, with tall posts, a long stair and a guard all the way around. Stepping down to a second level closer to grade often means shorter posts, less railing and a space that feels connected to the yard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a pergola on a deck need a permit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, yes, because it's a structure that adds load and wind forces to the deck. Towns handle it differently, so we include it in the deck permit or file it separately as your building department requires."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a pergola be attached to my existing deck?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only if the posts can carry down to proper footings. We don't bolt pergola posts to the deck boards or the side of a joist. On an existing deck that usually means opening the decking, adding footings below frost depth and running the posts down through the frame."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a built-in bench take the place of a railing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by itself. If the deck is high enough to need a guard, a bench along the edge becomes something to stand on, and many inspectors measure guard height from the seat. We either build the guard at the right height behind the bench or keep benches away from the drop."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do built-in planters rot the deck?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They can if soil and water sit against the framing. We line planters, give them drainage, and hold them off the deck surface so water drains away and air can move underneath."}}]}</script><!-- First-touch attribution. Runs before anything else so a click-id is never
